"Introducing Instella: New State-of-the-art Fully Open 3B Language Models — ROCm Blogs (https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/introducing-instella-3B/README.html)\nciteturn2451view0 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/introducing-instella-3B/README.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 265\nL0: Back to top [Input] [Input]\nL1: \nL2: [Input: Search...] `Ctrl`+`K`\nL3: \nL4: # Introducing Instella: New State-of-the-art Fully Open 3B Language Models\nL5: \nL6: ## Contents\nL7: # Introducing Instella: New State-of-the-art Fully Open 3B Language Modelscite0†# L8: \nL9: cite1†Image: Introducing Instella: New State-of-the-art Fully Open 3B Language Models L10: \nL11: March 05, 2025 by cite2†Jiang Liu , cite3†Jialian Wu , cite4†Xiaodong Yu , cite5†Prakamya Mishra , cite6†Sudhanshu Ranjan , cite7†Zicheng Liu , cite8†Chaitanya Manem , cite9†Yusheng Su , cite10†Pratik Prabhanjan Brahma , cite11†Gowtham Ramesh , cite12†Ximeng Sun , cite13†Ze Wang , cite14†Emad Barsoum .\nL12: \nL13: 7 min read. | 1627 total words.\nL14: \nL15: cite15†Applications & models L16: cite16†AI/ML , cite17†Fine-Tuning , cite18†Hardware L17: \nL18: cite19†AI L19: \nL20: AMD is excited to announce Instella, a family of fully open state-of-the-art 3-billion-parameter language models (LMs) trained from scratch on AMD Instinct™ MI300X GPUs. Instella models outperform existing fully open models of similar sizes and achieve competitive performance compared to state-of-the-art open-weight models such as Llama-3.2-3B, Gemma-2-2B, and Qwen-2.5-3B, including their instruction-tuned counterparts.\nL21: Our journey with Instella builds upon the foundation laid by our previous 1-billion-parameter LMs, cite20†AMD OLMo†www.amd.com which helped showcase the feasibility of training LMs end-to-end on AMD GPUs. With Instella, we have scaled our efforts by transitioning from a 1-billion-parameter model trained on 64 AMD Instinct MI250 GPUs using 1.3T tokens to a 3-billion-parameter model trained on 128 Instinct MI300X GPUs using 4.15T tokens.\nL22: While we compared our previous model with similarly sized fully open models only, Instella not only surpasses existing fully open models but also achieves overall competitive performance as compared to state-of-the-art open-weight models (Figure 1 cite21†[1] .), marking a significant step in bridging this gap.\nL23: cite22†Image: Scaling performance L24: \nL25: Figure 1: Comparing Instella Performance: Pareto frontier of pre-training tokens vs average performance for pre-trained and instruction-tuned models.cite23†# L26: By training Instella from scratch on Instinct MI300X GPUs, we highlight our hardware’s capability and scalability in handling demanding AI training workloads, offering a viable alternative in the AI hardware landscape.\nL27: In line with AMD’s commitment to open source, we are releasing all artifacts related to Instella models cite24†here , including the model weights, detailed training configurations, datasets, and code, enabling the AI community to collaborate, replicate, and innovate, thereby accelerating progress.\nL28: This blog will introduce you to our new family of Instella LMs. You will find out how to access these new models, learn, in details, how we trained them, and see how AMD’s new Instella LMs benchmark with other models. Follow the cite24†Additional Resources section to get started with using Instella models.\nL29: ## Takeawayscite25†# L30: \nL31:   * Announcing Instella, a series of 3 billion parameter language models developed by AMD, trained from scratch on 128 Instinct MI300X GPUs.\nL32: \nL33:   * Instella models significantly outperform existing fully open LMs (Figure 1) of comparable size, as well as bridge the gap between fully open and open weight models by achieving competitive performance compared state-of-the-art open weight models and their instruction-tuned counterparts.\nL34:   * Fully open and accessible: Fully open-source release of model weights, training hyperparameters, datasets, and code, fostering innovation and collaboration within the AI community.\nL35: \nL36:   * Supported by the AMD ROCm software stack, Instella employs efficient training techniques such as FlashAttention-2, Torch Compile, and Fully Sharded Data Parallelism (FSDP) with hybrid sharding to scale model training over a large cluster.\nL37: ## Instella Modelscite26†# L38: \nL39: In this release, we introduce the following Instella models (Table 2):\nL40: \nL41: Table 1: Instella models and training stages.\nL42: Model  | Stage  | Training Data (Tokens)  | Description\nL43: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL44: cite27†Instella-3B-Stage1†huggingface.co | Pre-training (Stage 1)  | 4.065 Trillion  | First stage pre-training to develop proficiency in natural language.\nL45: cite28†Instella-3B†huggingface.co | Pre-training (Stage 2)  | 57.575 Billion  | Second stage pre-training to further enhance problem solving capabilities.\nL46: cite29†Instella-3B-SFT†huggingface.co | SFT  | 8.902 Billion (x3 epochs)  | Supervised Fine-tuning (SFT) to enable instruction-following capabilities.\nL47: cite30†Instella-3B-Instruct†huggingface.co | DPO  | 760 Million  | Alignment to human preferences and strengthen chat capabilities with direct preference optimization (DPO).\nL48:  | Total:  | 4.15 Trillion  |\nL49: The Instella models are text-only, autoregressive transformer-based LMs having 3 billion parameters. Architecture-wise, Instella is packed with 36 decoder layers, each having 32 attention heads. These models support a sequence length of up to 4,096 tokens and have a vocabulary size of ~50,000 tokens using the OLMo tokenizercite31†[2] .\nL50: During both pre-training and fine-tuning, we utilized FlashAttention-2cite32†[3] , Torch Compile, and bfloat16 mixed-precision training to reduce memory usage, leading to computational speedups and optimal resource utilization. To balance inter-node memory efficiency and intra-node communication overhead within our cluster, we employed fully sharded data parallelism (FSDP) with hybrid sharding, with model parameters, gradients, and optimizer states sharded within a node and replicated across the nodes.\nL51: Our training pipeline is based on the open-sourced OLMo codebase, adapted, and optimized for our hardware and model architecture. For pre-training we used a total of 128 Instinct MI300X GPUs distributed across 16 nodes with each node having 8x Instinct MI300X GPUs. We evaluated our models and baselines using standard tasks from cite33†OLMES†github.com , cite34†FastChat MT-Bench†github.com , and cite35†Alpaca†github.com .\nL52: For more details about the architecture, training hyperparameters and evaluations, please refer to our cite28†huggingface model card†huggingface.co and our cite36†Github repository†github.com .\nL53: ## Training Pipelinecite37†# L54: \nL55: cite38†Image: Training pipeline L56: \nL57: Figure 2: Instella model training pipeline.cite39†# L58: The training of the Instella models comprised of four stages (Figure 2), where each stage incrementally enhanced the model’s capabilities from fundamental natural language understanding to instruction following and alignment towards human preferences. In this section we will briefly present Instella’s two pre-training stages and two instruction tuning & alignment stages, and their benchmark results.\nL59: ### Two stage pre-trainingcite40†# L60: In the first pre-training stage, we trained the model from scratch on 4.065 trillion tokens sourced from cite41†OLMoE-mix-0924†huggingface.co cite42†[4] , which is a diverse mix of two high-quality datasets cite43†DCLM-baseline†huggingface.co cite44†[5] and cite45†Dolma 1.7†huggingface.co cite46†[6] covering domains like coding, academics, mathematics, and general world knowledge from web crawl. This extensive first stage pre-training established a foundational understanding of general language in our Instella model.\nL61: For our final pre-trained checkpoint, cite28†Instella-3B†huggingface.co , we conducted a second stage pre-training on top of the first-stage cite27†Instella-3B-Stage1†huggingface.co model to further enhance its capabilities specifically in MMLU, BBH, and GSM8k.\nL62: To accomplish this, we further trained the model on an additional 57.575 billion tokens sourced from high-quality and diverse datasets, specifically from cite47†Dolmino-Mix-1124†huggingface.co cite31†[2] , cite48†SmolLM-Corpus (python-edu)†huggingface.co cite49†[7] , the cite50†Deepmind Mathematics†github.com cite51†[8] , and conversational datasets including cite52†Tülu-3-SFT-Mixture†huggingface.co cite53†[9] , cite54†OpenHermes-2.5†huggingface.co cite55†[10] , cite56†WebInstructSub†huggingface.co cite57†[11] , cite58†Code-Feedback†huggingface.co cite59†[12] , and cite60†Ultrachat 200k†huggingface.co cite61†[13] .\nL63: In addition to these publicly available datasets, 28.5 million tokens out of our second stage pre-training data-mix were derived from our in-house synthetic dataset focusing on mathematical problems.\nL64: This synthetic dataset was generated using the training set of GSM8k dataset, where we first used Qwen2.5-72B-Instruct to 1) Abstract numerical values as function parameters and generate a Python program to solve the math question, 2) Identify and replace numerical values in the existing question with alternative values that are still answerable with the same python program solution as the original question.\nL65: Next, by assigning different new values to these Python parameters and using the abstract solution program to compute the corresponding answers, we expanded our synthetic dataset with new and reliable question-answer pairscite62†[14] . The conversational datasets in this second stage pre-training data-mix were reformatted by concatenating question-answer pairs to be used for pre-training.\nL66: We trained the model on this data-mix three times with different random seeds and combined the model weights to obtain the final pre-trained model, cite28†Instella-3B†huggingface.co .\nL67: Table 2: Pre-trained model performance on standard benchmarks. Here Bold represents the best performance, and Underscore represents the second best performance.\nL68: Models  | Size  | Training Tokens  | Avg  | ARC Challenge  | ARC Easy  | BoolQ  | Hellaswag  | PiQA  | SciQ  | Winnograde  | OpenBookQA  | MMLU  | BBH (3-shot)  | GSM8k (8-shot)\nL69: --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | ---\nL70: Open Weight Models\nL71: ---\nL72: Gemma-2-2B  | 2.61B  | ~2T  | 59.34  | 39.46  | 59.30  | 74.50  | 70.50  | 76.40  | 96.60  | 69.80  | 44.80  | 53.28  | 40.75  | 27.37\nL73: Llama-3.2-3B  | 3.21B  | ~9T  | 62.51  | 47.16  | 64.91  | 74.80  | 73.10  | 75.90  | 95.30  | 70.30  | 51.20  | 57.81  | 47.00  | 30.10\nL74: Qwen2.5-3B  | 3.09B  | ~18T  | 68.30  | 51.51  | 67.19  | 79.10  | 72.10  | 77.40  | 95.50  | 69.30  | 51.40  | 67.22  | 56.69  | 63.84\nL75: Fully Open Models\nL76: ---\nL77: Pythia-2.8b  | 2.91B  | 300B  | 49.83  | 40.47  | 60.70  | 64.80  | 60.10  | 72.50  | 89.70  | 60.80  | 42.60  | 26.09  | 27.69  | 2.73\nL78: GPTNeo-2.7B  | 2.72B  | ~420B  | 47.96  | 38.46  | 54.56  | 62.70  | 55.20  | 70.80  | 88.00  | 58.30  | 40.80  | 27.83  | 27.25  | 3.71\nL79: OpenELM-3B  | 3.04B  | ~1.5T  | 52.28  | 37.46  | 58.42  | 68.60  | 71.70  | 75.60  | 92.50  | 65.40  | 46.40  | 26.69  | 29.40  | 2.96\nL80: StableLM-3B-4E1T  | 2.8B  | ~4T  | 58.51  | 44.82  | 67.02  | 75.40  | 74.20  | 78.40  | 93.40  | 68.40  | 48.60  | 45.19  | 37.33  | 10.84\nL81: cite27†Instella-3B-Stage1†huggingface.co | 3.11B  | ~4T  | 61.33  | 53.85  | 73.16  | 78.70  | 74.20  | 77.50  | 94.90  | 71.20  | 51.40  | 54.69  | 34.30  | 10.77\nL82: cite28†Instella-3B†huggingface.co | 3.11B  | ~4T+60B  | 66.59  | 52.84  | 70.53  | 76.50  | 75.00  | 77.80  | 96.40  | 73.10  | 52.40  | 58.31  | 39.74  | 59.82\nL83: #### Pre-training Resultscite63†# L84: \nL85:   * Both Instella-3B-Stage1 & Instella-3B models outperform all the other fully open models over all the benchmarks individually (except PIQA) (Table 2). Our final pre-trained checkpoint Instella-3B outperforms the existing top performant fully open pre-trained models by a lead of ⬆️8.08% on average, with significant improvements in `ARC Challenge [+8.02%], ARC Easy [+3.51%], Winnograde [+4.7%], OpenBookQA [+3.88%], MMLU [+13.12%] and ️GSM8K [+48.98%]`.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing Instella-Long: A Fully Open Language Model with Long-Context Capability — ROCm Blogs (https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/instella-long-context/README.html)\nciteturn2451view1 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: 2 days ago; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/instella-long-context/README.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 235\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nIntroducing Instella-Math: Fully Open Language Model with Reasoning Capability — ROCm Blogs (https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/instella-math-language/README.html)\nciteturn2451view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: yesterday; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://rocm.blogs.amd.com/artificial-intelligence/instella-math-language/README.html\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 137\n"
